# Ticket Routing

In this notebook you improve a bank support ticket classifier with labeled
examples. `:labeled_few_shot` copies a seeded sample of training tickets into
the prompt as demonstrations, and you then compare the original and tuned
classifier on held-out tickets. The notebook runs offline with no
credentials: a scripted model stands in for the provider.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"},
  {:plug, "~> 1.20"}
])
```

## Script the model

`Scripted` serves OpenAI-compatible replies through an in-process Plug, so
ReqLLM runs its real request path without a network call. `options/1` takes a
function of the decoded request body, which lets the reply depend on what the
prompt contains. In real use you pass a real model spec and the host's
credentials instead.

```elixir
defmodule Scripted do
  @moduledoc "Serves scripted OpenAI-compatible replies to ReqLLM through an in-process Plug."

  def model, do: %{provider: :openai, id: "gpt-4-turbo", extra: %{wire: %{protocol: "openai_chat"}}}

  def options(reply) when is_function(reply, 1) do
    plug = fn conn ->
      {:ok, body, conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
      respond(conn, reply.(JSON.decode!(body)))
    end

    [api_key: "scripted", req_http_options: [plug: plug], max_retries: 0]
  end

  def chat(fields) do
    Enum.map_join(fields, "\n\n", fn {name, value} -> "[[ ## #{name} ## ]]\n#{value}" end) <>
      "\n\n[[ ## completed ## ]]"
  end

  def field(body, name) do
    text = body["messages"] |> Enum.filter(&(&1["role"] == "user")) |> List.last() |> Map.fetch!("content")

    case Regex.run(~r/\[\[ ## #{name} ## \]\]\n(.*?)(?=\n\n\[\[ ## |\n\nRespond with|\z)/s, text) do
      [_, value] -> value
      nil -> nil
    end
  end

  def demos?(body), do: Enum.any?(body["messages"], &(&1["role"] == "assistant"))

  defp respond(conn, text) do
    send_json(conn, 200, %{
      id: "scripted",
      model: "gpt-4-turbo",
      choices: [%{message: %{role: "assistant", content: text}, finish_reason: "stop"}],
      usage: %{prompt_tokens: 10, completion_tokens: 5, total_tokens: 15}
    })
  end

  defp send_json(conn, status, body) do
    conn
    |> Plug.Conn.put_resp_content_type("application/json")
    |> Plug.Conn.send_resp(status, JSON.encode!(body))
  end
end
```

The scripted router answers `"unrouted"` when the prompt has no
demonstrations. With demonstrations, it routes by keywords. This mimics a
model that needs examples to learn the label set.

```elixir
route = fn ticket ->
  cond do
    ticket =~ ~r/lost|missing|find my/i -> "lost_card"
    ticket =~ ~r/atm|cash/i -> "cash_withdrawal"
    ticket =~ ~r/fee/i -> "card_payment_fee"
    true -> "unrouted"
  end
end

reply = fn body ->
  label = if Scripted.demos?(body), do: route.(Scripted.field(body, "ticket")), else: "unrouted"
  Scripted.chat(label: label)
end

model = [
  model: Scripted.model(),
  adapter: Tune.Adapter.Chat,
  request_options: Scripted.options(reply)
]
```

## Define the classifier and data

The output is a `Zoi.enum/1`, so a reply outside the label set fails
validation instead of reaching your code.

```elixir
{:ok, classifier} =
  Tune.Predictor.new(
    signature: [
      instructions: "Route the support ticket.",
      inputs: [ticket: Zoi.string()],
      outputs: [label: Zoi.enum(["lost_card", "cash_withdrawal", "card_payment_fee", "unrouted"])]
    ]
  )

rows = fn pairs ->
  Enum.map(pairs, fn {id, ticket, label} ->
    %{id: id, input: %{ticket: ticket}, expected: %{label: label}}
  end)
end

train =
  rows.([
    {"train-1", "I lost my debit card on the train", "lost_card"},
    {"train-2", "My card is missing after a trip", "lost_card"},
    {"train-3", "The ATM debited me and dispensed no cash", "cash_withdrawal"},
    {"train-4", "Cash withdrawal failed at the station ATM", "cash_withdrawal"},
    {"train-5", "There is a fee on my card purchase abroad", "card_payment_fee"},
    {"train-6", "Why was there a fee on my card payment?", "card_payment_fee"}
  ])

test =
  rows.([
    {"lost-card", "I cannot find my card", "lost_card"},
    {"no-cash", "The ATM did not give me cash", "cash_withdrawal"},
    {"purchase-fee", "Why is there a purchase fee?", "card_payment_fee"}
  ])

:ok
```

## Build the study

The `:route` score gives 1 for the expected label and 0 otherwise.
`:labeled_few_shot` with `k: 4` samples four training tickets. The seed fixes
the sample, so the same seed gives the same demonstrations.

```elixir
study =
  Tune.new(classifier, model: model)
  |> Tune.data(train: train, test: test)
  |> Tune.score(:route, fn row, output ->
    if output["label"] == row.expected["label"], do: 1, else: 0
  end)
  |> Tune.search(:labeled_few_shot, k: 4)
  |> Tune.limit(seed: 5, model_calls: 12)

{:ok, original_prompt} = Tune.preview(study, %{ticket: "I cannot find my card"})
Enum.map(original_prompt.context.messages, & &1.role)
```

`Tune.preview/2` renders the request without a model call. The original
prompt has only a system message and the ticket:

```text
[:system, :user]
```

## Select demonstrations

```elixir
{:ok, winner} = Tune.tune(study)

for demo <- winner.value.demos do
  IO.puts("#{demo.inputs["ticket"]} -> #{demo.outputs["label"]}")
end

IO.puts("Search model calls: #{Enum.sum(Map.values(winner.calls))}")
```

```text
Why was there a fee on my card payment? -> card_payment_fee
The ATM debited me and dispensed no cash -> cash_withdrawal
I lost my debit card on the train -> lost_card
Cash withdrawal failed at the station ATM -> cash_withdrawal
Search model calls: 0
```

`:labeled_few_shot` makes no model calls and computes no score during
search. It only copies complete labeled rows. `winner.calls` is empty, and
`winner.metadata.notes` records how many demonstrations each predictor
received.

```elixir
Enum.sort(winner.metadata.notes)
```

```text
[
  demos: %{"root" => 4},
  settings: %{"k" => 4, "sample" => true},
  train_duplicates_removed: 0
]
```

Training rows with the same input count once. `train_duplicates_removed`
reports how many were dropped.

## Inspect the tuned prompt

`winner.value` is the tuned classifier. Preview it with the same model
configuration. Each demonstration adds one user and one assistant message
before the ticket.

```elixir
{:ok, tuned_prompt} = Tune.new(winner.value, model: model) |> Tune.preview(%{ticket: "I cannot find my card"})

Enum.map(tuned_prompt.context.messages, & &1.role)
```

```text
[:system, :user, :assistant, :user, :assistant, :user, :assistant, :user,
 :assistant, :user]
```

## Compare on held-out tickets

`Tune.compare/3` runs both classifiers on the test split. It pairs the
results by case ID.

```elixir
{:ok, comparison} = Tune.compare(study, winner, split: :test)

Enum.sort(comparison.metrics.route)
```

```text
[
  baseline_mean: 0.0,
  challenger_mean: 1.0,
  counts: %{improved: 3},
  delta: 1.0,
  direction: :maximize,
  paired_count: 3,
  status: :improved
]
```

Each row holds both observations. The original classifier routed the lost
card ticket to `"unrouted"`; the tuned one routed it correctly.

```elixir
row = comparison.rows["lost-card"]

IO.puts("original: #{row.baseline.output["label"]}")
IO.puts("tuned:    #{row.challenger.output["label"]}")
row.metrics.route
```

```text
original: unrouted
tuned:    lost_card
```

```text
%{status: :improved, delta: 1, baseline: 0, challenger: 1}
```

The held-out tickets never appear in the prompt. The tuned classifier scores
1.0 on them because the scripted model needs examples of each label, not
copies of the test tickets.

